{% figura src="/images/vignettes/issa-17.png" alt="Line drawing of a room plan with two turntables and a mixer at the centre and figures standing around them" /%}

*Visual Broadcasters & Club Streamers: The booth.*

Boiler Room pioneered the visual DJ streaming format in March 2010, in Dalston, East London, forever altering how underground dance music is consumed. Founded by Blaise Bellville and Thristian Richards, the platform began as a raw digital experiment when the creators duct-taped a consumer webcam to a concrete wall inside an abandoned boiler room. Using the nascent live-streaming platform Ustream, they broadcasted a live vinyl set by Thristian to a small online audience, effectively bridging the gap between underground pirate radio culture and the digital age. In the original boiler room, it was natural that because of the small space, the DJ sat at eye level with the webcam, and the audience behind them. It was a dark, literally underground space, where the necessities consisted of the table, the turntable, a mixer, and a webcam. It was here that the booth typology was born, which today is immediately identifiable by a few elements: a formation of bodies moving in a dark space, the deck in front and center, and the DJ. In tandem, they have also each developed their own identity, mainly through recognizable backgrounds like the sticker covered container of The Lot, the neon green tiles of HOR, the calming bar atmosphere of Le Mellotron, or the eclectic backgrounds of [login.jp](http://login.jp), which broadcasts from places such as a fish market or a farm. These are just a few, dispersed around the world, and united by the booth.

